# Rust Cargo and Feature Rules

## Scope

This file owns native Cargo manifest, dependency, and feature-gate policy. Keep
runtime error policy in `error_handling.mdc`, FFI safety in `rust_ffi.mdc`, and
test execution guidance in `rust_testing.mdc`.

## Manifests

- Keep `Cargo.toml` focused on package metadata, dependencies, features, tests,
  benches, and build configuration.
- Move release history, sprint notes, and long migration narratives to
  `CHANGELOG.md` or native docs instead of growing manifests.
- Keep workspace-level settings in `native/Cargo.toml` when they apply to every
  native crate.
- Keep crate-level settings in the crate `Cargo.toml` when they apply only to
  that crate.

## Dependencies

- Prefer the smallest dependency set that keeps code clearer and safer.
- Avoid adding a dependency for simple helpers that are easier to maintain
  locally.
- Make optional dependencies explicit with `dep:` feature entries.
- Document why platform-specific or dynamically loaded dependencies exist.
- Review default features before accepting them; disable defaults when the
  crate only needs a narrow capability.

## Features

- Keep features additive and narrowly scoped to the capability they enable.
- Use feature names that describe behavior, not implementation accidents.
- Keep feature-gated code local to the module or adapter that owns the optional
  behavior.
- Cross-platform features must either work on supported platforms or degrade to
  a documented no-op or typed unsupported error.
- Add or update tests for feature-gated behavior when a feature changes.

## Lockfile and Build Outputs

- Treat `Cargo.lock` as part of the native build contract for this repository.
- Do not commit generated build outputs from `target/` or ad-hoc native binaries
  unless the repository intentionally tracks that artifact.
- When dependency versions change, verify the affected native build and tests at
  the narrowest practical scope.
